Day 50 & 51 of my 120-Day Data Analytics Challenge 

Case Study 5 (Data Mart) was a fun one… but also a real test of analytical thinking.

The company rolled out eco-friendly packaging in June 2020, and the big question was simple:

Did it help, hurt, or just confuse the sales numbers?

To answer that, I dug into the dataset to:
• Clean and prepare the data for analysis
• Compare trends before vs after the packaging change (12-week window)
• Break performance down by region, platform, and customer segments
• Identify where things actually shifted

Key Findings:

📈 Segments that performed well
• Families (Demographic): stronger post change sales contribution
• Middle Aged customers:  stable and resilient
• Retail platform:  continued to dominate overall performance

📉 Segments that struggled
• Couples (Demographic):  noticeable decline in sales
• Young Adults:  weaker engagement after the switch
• Shopify platform:  lagged behind Retail in growth

What this means:
The sustainable packaging change may have aligned well with family-oriented and practical buyers,
but didn’t resonate as strongly with younger or couple-based segments.

 Recommendations for Danny’s Team

1. Segment-Specific Messaging
• Emphasize sustainability benefits for Families
• Reframe value (price, convenience, lifestyle) for Young Adults & Couples

2. Improve Shopify Experience
• Review UX, pricing visibility, and checkout flow
• Clearly communicate the value of the packaging change online

3. Build a Customer Feedback Loop
• Gather feedback from underperforming segments

4. Test Before Full Rollout
• Measure impact across segments before scaling future changes

Looking at total sales alone would have hidden all of this.

But breaking it down revealed something powerful

